  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">Scope: all six routes. Fail closed. A missing caller oid is the existing 401 (it does not depend on the record). A missing bearer token, an unresolvable Dataverse user, an anchor read that faults, a RetrievePrincipalAccess fault, a privilege query that fails, a playbook or node lookup that faults or returns null, an `IConsumerRoutingService` fault, a missing registration of any service a check needs, or any exception in a check DENIES. There is no app-only fallback on any path. The `AnalysisAuthorizationFilter.InvokeAsync` switch default (:110) must DENY (403 with reasonCode `sdap.access.error.system_failure`), not call next.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">Scope: unknown versus denied. On every route, a target that does not exist and a target the caller cannot access get the IDENTICAL response (status, title, detail, reasonCode, extension keys; only correlation/trace ids differ). No response body or caller-visible log line contains the requested analysis id. For the three analysis-id routes the answer is `FinanceAuthorizationFilter.UniformRecordNotFound`, and the handlers' `KeyNotFoundException` branches (:587-590, :647-650, :674-677) return the SAME result (add `HttpContext` to the GET handler's parameters to build it), so an analysis deleted between check and handler is indistinguishable too. For body ids (fork, promote, execute) the answer is the evaluator's `FinanceDenial.Forbidden` 403 (`ProblemDetailsHelper.Forbidden(reasonCode, traceId)`); because `DataverseAccessDataSource` answers None for a missing row, a missing and a denied record both produce reasonCode `sdap.access.deny.insufficient_rights`. A playbook that `GetPlaybookAsync` does not find is checked with the same Record-path check as a non-public one (Dataverse answers None for it), so it gets the same 403. Promote's not-your-session is the existing 404 `{ error = "Session not found", correlationId }` body, identical to a missing session.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-008 / project">Scope: placement. Each route's check runs in an endpoint filter in that route's own fluent chain, BEFORE the handler. Do NOT put caller checks inside AnalysisOrchestrationService, AnalysisDocumentLoader, WorkingDocumentService, ChatSessionManager, PlaybookOrchestrationService or any node executor: those run on app-only paths too (for example `Services/Ai/AppOnlyAnalysisService.cs` and background jobs), which have no caller. ONE exception, by the #863 precedent: promote's session-derived checks (session owner, and the session's own DocumentId when the body has none) run in the PromoteSession handler, after `GetSessionAsync` (:1344) and before `CreateAnalysisAsync` (:1405), exactly where fork does its owner check, and the SessionOwnershipGuardTests row says so. The owner comparison runs IMMEDIATELY after `GetSessionAsync`, before the already-bound 400 (:1351-1359), so another user's bound session answers 404, not 400.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11 / project">Scope: how checks are evaluated. Every NEW check in this task is a `FinanceAuthorizationCheck` evaluated by `FinanceAuthorizationFilter`: AnalysisAuthorizationFilter builds the check list for the request (asynchronously where it must read Dataverse), wraps it in `FinanceAuthorizationTargets.Authorize(...)` or `FinanceAuthorizationTargets.Reject(...)`, and returns `await new FinanceAuthorizationFilter(services.GetRequiredService&lt;AuthorizationService&gt;(), _ =&gt; targets, denial, services.GetService&lt;CallerRecordAccessProbe&gt;()).InvokeAsync(context, next)`. Do NOT edit FinanceAuthorizationFilter.cs and do NOT write a second loop that calls AuthorizeAsync / GetCallerRecordAccessAsync / CallerHoldsPrivilegeAsync and turns the answers into responses. The only direct `AuthorizationService.AuthorizeAsync` call allowed is promote's session-derived document check in the handler (see the promote constraint), which renders its deny with the same `ProblemDetailsHelper.Forbidden(FinanceAuthorizationFilter.InsufficientRightsReasonCode, traceId: …)` body. The existing DocumentAccess mode (`IAiAuthorizationService`) is unchanged for /create and /execute.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the AnalysisAccess mode (GET, export, save). (1) Read the analysis's anchor ids app-only with ONE `IGenericEntityService.RetrieveAsync("sprk_analysis", analysisId, [anchor columns])`. Select only lookup columns verified to exist live (step 0); a column that does not exist makes Dataverse fault every read, which would deny every honest caller. (2) A not-found fault (`RecordContainerResolver.IsRecordNotFound`) and any other fault both deny with the uniform 404 (return `FinanceAuthorizationTargets.Reject(FinanceAuthorizationFilter.UniformRecordNotFound(httpContext))`). (3) The anchor columns are every Lookup on sprk_analysis that targets a record that carries content or confers access (document, matter, project, work assignment, invoice, budget, communication, event, or any other record type the live sweep finds), including the core-ancestor stamp columns. Lookups to configuration (sprk_playbook, sprk_agreementtype, sprk_regardingrecordtype → sprk_recordtype_ref, transactioncurrencyid) or to principals and business units (ownerid, createdby, modifiedby, createdonbehalfby, modifiedonbehalfby, owningbusinessunit, owningteam, owninguser) are NOT anchors. The classification is ONE static table in AnalysisAuthorizationFilter.cs naming every Lookup the live sweep returns, each marked anchor or non-anchor; a test fails on an unclassified column. (4) Required: the caller holds Read on EVERY populated anchor (AND, not OR). Each document anchor (any lookup targeting sprk_document) is a `FinanceCheckPath.Document` check with operation "read" against `FinanceAuthorizationFilter.DocumentEntitySet`; every other anchor is a `FinanceCheckPath.Record` check with operation "read" and the entity set from `EntityAccessFilter.TryResolveEntitySet`. Denial = `FinanceDenial.UniformNotFound`. (5) An analysis with NO populated anchor is denied (zero checks; the evaluator denies). The polymorphic pair (`sprk_regardingrecordid` / `sprk_regardingrecordtype`) is not an anchor: the typed lookup written with it is. An analysis whose only link is the pair is therefore denied. (6) An anchor whose type has no entity set in `EntityAccessFilter.TryResolveEntitySet` (for example sprk_budget, sprk_communication) is not given a guessed set: the request is denied with the uniform 404; see the escalation trigger for live rows that have one. (7) A missing or unparseable analysisId route value keeps the existing 400 (it does not depend on the record; the {analysisId:guid} route constraint makes it unreachable in practice).</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: rights per analysis-id route (decided at authoring). GET = Read on all anchors. Export = Read on all anchors: it discloses the same `sprk_workingdocument` text GET returns, so the SPE "download_file" = Write policy (OperationAccessPolicy.cs:140) does not apply; it governs SPE file bytes, not analysis text. Save = Read on all anchors, PLUS, only when step 0 shows `sprk_analysis.sprk_matterid` exists live, a Record check "entity.associate_document" (AppendTo) on that matter (entity set from `TryResolveEntitySet("sprk_matter")`), read in the same anchor retrieve, before the upload. If the column does not exist: record that the SPE branch of `WorkingDocumentService.SaveToSpeAsync` is unreachable, and leave its destination code UNCHANGED. Do not repoint it at sprk_regardingmatter: that would newly enable an app-only SPE write, which is a functional change outside this task. Save's no-container fallback rewrites the analysis's own stored text with itself (no caller-controlled content), so it needs no extra right.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner rounds 3b, 7 item 3, 9 (G5, binding)">Scope: fork and promote create sprk_analysis rows APP-ONLY, so they follow G5: check as the user, then the app writes. Add ONE policy key to OperationAccessPolicy.cs: `["analysis.attach"] = AccessRights.Read | AccessRights.AppendTo` (Read because fork binds a session whose active document is the target and its metadata enters the prompt; AppendTo because the new row's lookup points at the parent, the "finance.attach_invoice" reasoning). Add ONE constant to AnalysisAuthorizationFilter.cs for the Create privilege on sprk_analysis, spelled exactly as step 0 reads it from live `privileges` (the case of the table's schema name matters: task 130 found `prvCreatesprk_Invoice`), pinned by a unit test. Fork checks, in this order: `FinanceAuthorizationCheck.CallerPrivilege(&lt;that constant&gt;, "sprk_analyses", "privilege.create")`; a Document check "analysis.attach" on `AnalysisForkRequest.DocumentId`; when `PlaybookId` is non-null, the playbook-use decision. Promote checks, in this order: the Create privilege; a Document check "analysis.attach" on a non-empty body `DocumentId`; a Record check "analysis.attach" on `RegardingEntityId` when `RegardingEntityType` (trimmed, lower-cased, as the handler does) is sprk_matter or sprk_project (entity set from `TryResolveEntitySet`); when `PlaybookId` is non-null, the playbook-use decision. Denial = `FinanceDenial.Forbidden`. Every check names its source (for example "body.documentId") for the deny log.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: fork and promote validation order. The handlers' body validations stay where they are AND run first in the filter, so a malformed body gets the handler's existing 400 before any rights query: extract each handler's body validation into one static method in AnalysisEndpoints.cs (fork: priorSessionId, name, DocumentId; promote: sessionId, name, regarding shape) returning the same `AnalysisProblem` 400 or null, call it from the handler and from the filter's target declaration (`FinanceAuthorizationTargets.Reject(...)`), and do not change any 400 text. Promote with no body DocumentId and no regarding passes the filter with only the privilege check (and the playbook check when PlaybookId is set); the handler then checks the session-derived document. Replace `AddAiAuthorizationFilter()` on /fork and /promote with new extension methods `AddAnalysisForkAuthorizationFilter()` and `AddAnalysisPromoteAuthorizationFilter()` on `AnalysisAuthorizationFilterExtensions` (new `AuthorizationMode` values `AnalysisFork` and `AnalysisPromote`, appended after `AnalysisAccess`). Do not change `AiAuthorizationFilter` itself (task 164's surface).</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: promote, in the handler. (1) Right after `GetSessionAsync`: when the session exists and its `OwnerOid` is null or differs (ordinal) from the caller's `CallerResolution.ResolveObjectId`, return the existing 404 body and write nothing; a missing oid is the existing-style 401 `AnalysisProblem`. (2) When the body has no DocumentId and the session's DocumentId is used, call `AuthorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(new AuthorizationContext { UserId = oid, ResourceId = sessionDocId, Operation = "analysis.attach", CorrelationId, UserAccessToken = TokenHelper.ExtractBearerTokenOrNull(httpContext) })` before `CreateAnalysisAsync`; a deny or an exception returns `ProblemDetailsHelper.Forbidden(FinanceAuthorizationFilter.InsufficientRightsReasonCode, detail: &lt;the same constant detail the filter's 403 carries&gt;, traceId: …)` and writes nothing. (3) The session-derived PlaybookId (`session.PlaybookId`) is not checked here: it was chosen at session create, which is task 164's surface; record that in the note.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: the playbook-use decision ("may this caller use this playbook"), shared with task 164. Implement it ONCE as a public static async method on PlaybookAuthorizationFilter (for example `BuildPlaybookUseCheckAsync(IPlaybookService playbookService, Guid playbookId, string source, CancellationToken ct)`) that returns either "no check needed" (the playbook exists and `IsPublic` is true) or a `FinanceCheckPath.Record` check with operation "read" on `PlaybookService.EntitySetName` (made internal; never a second "sprk_analysisplaybooks" literal) for that id. A non-public playbook and a playbook `GetPlaybookAsync` does not find both get the Record check, so Dataverse's own answer decides: ownership, team POA shares and role depth (owner round 9: the caller's own Dataverse rights decide). A `GetPlaybookAsync` fault denies. Do NOT reuse the OwnerOrSharedOrPublic branch of `InvokeAsync` (it compares an oid with a systemuserid) and do NOT change `InvokeAsync`, `AddPlaybookOwnerAuthorizationFilter` or `AddPlaybookAccessAuthorizationFilter` in this task: those routes are 164's, and 164 switches them to this method.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: execute. (1) Keep `AddAnalysisExecuteAuthorizationFilter()` on /execute exactly as it is (Read on every `DocumentIds` entry through `IAiAuthorizationService`), and chain a NEW `AddAnalysisRunAuthorizationFilter()` (new `AuthorizationMode` value `AnalysisRun`) after it, so the Read check runs first. /create's chain is not touched. (2) When `PlaybookId` is null, the run filter returns the handler's existing 400 body itself (move the message text to one constant used by both; the body stays `{ error = … }` with status 400), making no lookup. (3) Document-profile branch: resolve the profile playbook id with the SAME call the handler makes (`IConsumerRoutingService.ResolveAsync(ConsumerTypes.DocumentProfile)`, :333). When it equals `request.PlaybookId`, require a Document check "write" on every document in `DocumentIds` (Denial Forbidden). The profile playbook is the system Binding's, not caller-chosen, so it gets no playbook-use check. (4) Engine branch (every other PlaybookId): the caller must pass the playbook-use decision for `request.PlaybookId`. (5) Engine branch: read the nodes with `INodeService.GetNodesAsync(request.PlaybookId)`, the same source PlaybookOrchestrationService uses. Require a Document check "write" on EVERY document in `DocumentIds` when ANY of these holds: the node array is empty (Legacy mode writes the document's profile outputs and enqueues indexing, AnalysisOrchestrationService.cs:988, :1032); any node, active or not, has a null `SprkExecutortype`; any node's `SprkExecutortype` is side-effecting. (6) A node executor is side-effecting when it creates or updates a Dataverse row, writes to SPE, enqueues indexing or a job, sends a message (email, Teams, notification, webhook), or delegates to an external agent or tool runtime whose actions the BFF does not control (for example AgentService). Classify by reading each executor registered in `NodeExecutorRegistry`. The classification is ONE closed `IReadOnlySet&lt;ExecutorType&gt;` (or equivalent) in `Services/Ai/Nodes/INodeExecutor.cs` next to `ExecutorType`, with a one-line reason per side-effecting value; a test enumerates `Enum.GetValues&lt;ExecutorType&gt;()` and fails on any value that is neither listed as side-effecting nor listed as read-only. If task 164 has already landed such a list, reuse it. (7) Execute's documents are the only Write targets; this task does not add per-node rights on the records a node writes (recorded in the note).</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11">Scope: new surface. No new filter class, no new service, no new DI registration and no new logical-name → entity-set map. The only new surface is: three `AuthorizationMode` values with their three extension methods (AnalysisFork, AnalysisPromote, AnalysisRun) in AnalysisAuthorizationFilter.cs; the anchor classification table and the Create-privilege constant in the same file; the "analysis.attach" policy key; the playbook-use static method on PlaybookAuthorizationFilter; and the side-effect classification next to ExecutorType. Each carries the three-question justification in the PR with grep evidence. Do NOT add entries to `EntityAccessFilter.EntitySetByType` (it has a lockstep invariant with DocumentAssociationMap) or to `SemanticSearchAuthorizationFilter.AuthorizableEntitySets` (that widens POST /api/ai/search). Entity-set names are never derived by pluralizing; use `TryResolveEntitySet`, the FinanceAuthorizationFilter constants, `PlaybookService.EntitySetName`, and "sprk_analyses" only as the Privilege check's log label (it reaches no URL).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-019 / ADR-003">Scope: every new deny. Carry a machine-readable reasonCode in ProblemDetails; the evaluator's constants (`sdap.access.deny.insufficient_rights`, `sdap.access.deny.insufficient_privilege`, `sdap.access.error.system_failure`, `sdap.access.deny.no_target`, `sdap.access.deny.record_unavailable`) are the only ones used. The fork/promote/execute 403 detail is one constant string that names no record (HistoryOverlay shows `detail` to the user). The existing DocumentAccess 403 shape for /create and the existing execute Read deny stay as they are.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-002">Scope: whole task. No Dataverse plugins. Every decision is made in the BFF at request time.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Scope: tests added or changed. No Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;, no DI-registration tests, no constructor null-check tests. Substitute at the IAccessDataSource boundary (reuse task 130's `RecordingAccessDataSource`), the CallerRecordAccessProbe virtual privilege seam (reuse `RecordingPrivilegeProbe`), and the IGenericEntityService, IConsumerRoutingService, IPlaybookService, INodeService, IAiAuthorizationService, IAnalysisOrchestrationService, IAnalysisDataverseService, IPlaybookOrchestrationService and ChatSessionManager-store boundaries, following the existing contract fixtures; do not write third copies of the recording fakes. The deny tests go through the REAL `MapAnalysisEndpoints` in-process host with the real `AuthorizationService`: a test that only invokes the filter class directly, or only asserts a mock was called, does not count as reach.</constraint>
    <constraint source="task 074 / owner round 9 item 3">Scope: RouteAuthorizationGuardTests. If task 167 is on this branch: delete every Pending waiver it added for these six routes (NoWaiverIsStale forces this), keep Rule A, Rule B, the census and NoWaiverIsStale green, and add no new waiver for any route in AnalysisEndpoints.cs. If 167 is NOT on this branch: add `Api/Ai/AnalysisEndpoints.cs` to `GovernedFiles` with `Scope.RouteLevelGate` and a reason, add no waivers, and state in the task note and the PR that the main session reconciles with 167 at integration. Either way the note says which case applied. Do not change `FilterMarker` or `DecisionServices`.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: GitHub #233. Delete the "TRACKED: GitHub #233" placeholder (AnalysisAuthorizationFilter.cs:203-209) and the method remarks that call the mode a placeholder (:180-184). Correct the stale class remark (:69, "must own the analysis or have access to its source document") and the extension summaries (:15-18, :31-34) to the rules implemented. The PR says it closes #233 item 1 only; the issue stays open for its other items.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project hard gate">Scope: test runs before the PR. The affected tests, the FULL BFF unit suite, NetArchTest (tests/Spaarke.ArchTests), and BOTH integration suites run IN FULL: `dotnet test tests/integration/Sprk.Bff.Api.IntegrationTests` and `dotnet test tests/integration/Spe.Integration.Tests`, not just a build. No live Dataverse in CI.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: the PR. State the placement decision (in BFF: these are existing BFF routes and filters; no new service), citing `.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md`. Measure publish size against a FRESH build of origin/master from a short path, both sides zipped with Compress-Archive, equal file counts; report both absolute sizes and the delta; ceiling 60 MB. Run `dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive`: no new HIGH. Run `/conflict-check` before the PR.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner (binding)">Scope: whole task. No scope cuts and nothing deferred: scope found during execution (for example another caller-chosen id one of these handlers consumes) is added to this task. Live Dataverse is READ-ONLY during development: metadata, privilege and row-count queries are allowed; writes are not. The live gate is a manual gate run by the main session with the existing non-admin test users in their current business units. Do not relocate users and do not edit roles; if a gate needs a user at a specific access level, ask the owner to create one (owner round 4 item 1).</constraint>
  </constraints>

